# VideoFetch safe-egress deployment configuration — TEMPLATE ONLY.
#
# Install as /etc/videofetch/media-egress.env, owned by root, mode 0644.
#
# THIS FILE CONTAINS NO SECRET. A listening port and a DNS resolver address are
# non-secret deployment facts, and the file is deliberately world-readable so
# `systemctl show` and ordinary operators can inspect it. Never add a
# credential here.
#
# NOTHING HAS BEEN PROVISIONED. Every value below is intentionally empty. Empty
# is not a default: vf-egress-config-check refuses to start the boundary until
# each one is set, because guessing either value would either publish an
# unintended port or admit an unintended resolver.
#
# Read by BOTH systemd (as EnvironmentFile=) and the vf-egress-* helpers, which
# parse it with the same semantics rather than sourcing it as shell.

# The port the Worker listens on, published on VM LOOPBACK ONLY by
# videofetch-media-netns.service. It must equal WORKER_PORT in the Worker's own
# environment file, and it is what cloudflared connects to on 127.0.0.1.
VIDEOFETCH_WORKER_PORT=

# THE DESIGNATED DNS RESOLVER(S) — the single authoritative declaration.
#
# Written in container-runtime flag form, as one or more `--dns <address>`
# pairs, because ONE declaration feeds two consumers that must never disagree:
#
#   * videofetch-media-netns.service passes it to `docker run`, which is what
#     puts the resolver in the namespace's /etc/resolv.conf. The Worker
#     inherits that file and cannot override it, because docker rejects --dns
#     together with `--network container:`.
#
#   * vf-egress-policy-install renders the firewall's DNS exception from the
#     same string.
#
# Two separate settings would eventually drift, and the failure would be either
# a DNS outage that looks like a network fault or a firewall hole for a
# resolver nothing uses.
#
# EACH ADDRESS MUST BE EXACT. A prefix is rejected: `--dns 172.17.0.0/16` is
# refused, because safe-egress.md permits an exception for one resolver
# address, never for a private network. A resolver that happens to sit inside a
# denied class is fine and expected — it is admitted at that exact address on
# port 53 only, and the surrounding class stays denied.
#
# Example for a Docker bridge gateway resolver (quote the value; systemd and
# the helpers both strip one layer of quotes):
#   VIDEOFETCH_MEDIA_DNS_FLAGS="--dns 172.17.0.1"
#
# The prototype hard-coded 172.17.0.1 into its firewall source. That address is
# a property of one host at one moment, not a constant, so it is configuration
# here and is not committed.
VIDEOFETCH_MEDIA_DNS_FLAGS=
